# QUIC stack dependencies
#
# What implements RFC 9000 underneath the Nowhere protocol layer. Settled as
# D-15 by conformance/scripts/quic-probe.sh, which built exactly these two at
# exactly these revisions, exported keying material from them, and had a real
# Portal answer READY to the AuthFrame derived from it.
#
# This file is the single source of truth. Both the probe and the app's own
# native build read it, so the thing that was proven and the thing that ships
# cannot drift to different versions — which is a property of there being one
# file, not of a check that compares two.
#
# A tag is a name and a name can be moved. The commit is what is verified after
# fetching; a mismatch fails the build rather than warning.

NGTCP2_REPO=https://github.com/ngtcp2/ngtcp2
NGTCP2_TAG=v1.17.0
NGTCP2_COMMIT=01b05ba10d9560ff144e5cb9d2f7d3155906f31c
NGTCP2_LICENCE=MIT

AWSLC_REPO=https://github.com/aws/aws-lc
AWSLC_TAG=v1.68.0
AWSLC_COMMIT=062819084fcf9cb5bb02bd4389695d581a731ce0
AWSLC_LICENCE=Apache-2.0 OR ISC

# Why these two, and not the alternatives
#
# NW-P-01 authenticates a QUIC connection with an exporter under the label
# `EXPORTER-Nowhere-Auth`, so whatever provides QUIC must expose RFC 5705
# keying material from its TLS 1.3 handshake. That one line eliminates the
# obvious Android answer -- Cronet gives neither raw streams nor an exporter --
# and it is why the Apple client wrapped ngtcp2 in a TLS handler of its own
# rather than using the platform's QUIC, which it does not reference anywhere.
#
# ngtcp2 also never owns a socket: the application feeds it received datagrams
# and sends the ones it produces. Inside a VPN that is not a preference. Every
# outbound socket must be protect()-ed or it routes back into the TUN, so a
# library that opens its own socket is either hookable or unusable.
#
# aws-lc over BoringSSL, for one reason that outlives preference: aws-lc has
# versioned releases and BoringSSL does not, and a vendored dependency this
# repository has to track and upgrade on a schedule is worth more when it has a
# version number.
#
# Android needs no custom crypto backend. That is the Apple client's largest L3
# cost -- ngtcp2_crypto_apple.c plus a TLS 1.3 handshake written in Swift,
# because that platform ships no TLS a QUIC stack can drive -- and it does not
# recur here. The backend below is upstream's own, unmodified.

# What is deliberately not taken (rule 11)
#
# aws-lc:  BUILD_TESTING=OFF, BUILD_TOOL=OFF, BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF.
#          No test suite, no command-line tool, no shared object. What ships is
#          libssl.a and libcrypto.a, statically linked.
# ngtcp2:  ENABLE_LIB_ONLY=ON, ENABLE_SHARED_LIB=OFF, BUILD_TESTING=OFF,
#          ENABLE_OPENSSL=OFF. No examples, no client or server tool, no second
#          crypto backend. What ships is libngtcp2.a and the BoringSSL crypto
#          shim libngtcp2_crypto_boringssl.a.
#
# Neither is patched. If either ever is, the patch belongs in this directory
# next to this file, and this comment stops being true.

# Corresponding source (D-17)
#
# These are statically linked into a GPL-3.0 binary, so a distributed build must
# be accompanied by the source it was built from. `build-deps.sh --bundle-source`
# produces that archive from the verified trees. Fetching at a pinned commit is
# how the build stays reproducible; it is not by itself the obligation being
# met.
